  ('IMAGEM-015', 'Create an ultra-realistic product lifestyle photograph using all provided reference images.

PRODUCT REFERENCES:
The first 3 to 5 images are PRODUCT REFERENCE IMAGES. Each image represents the same product in a different color or variation. Accurately identify and reproduce each product shown in the references.

SCENE REFERENCE:
The final image is the ENVIRONMENT/SCENE REFERENCE. Preserve this environment as the main setting and use it as the visual foundation for the final composition.

TASK:
Place ALL referenced product variations together naturally inside the provided environment, creating a realistic lifestyle product photograph.

If the product reference images show the products being worn by a model, extract the PRODUCT itself from those references and reconstruct it naturally as a physical product placed in the scene. Do not include the model unless specifically requested.

PRODUCT ACCURACY IS THE HIGHEST PRIORITY:
- Preserve the exact product design, shape, proportions, cut, seams, stitching, fabric texture, straps, openings, folds and construction.
- Preserve each original color exactly as shown in the product references.
- Do not merge colors between products.
- Do not redesign, simplify or invent details.
- Do not change the product''s original model or structure.
- Each variation must remain visually distinguishable.
- Maintain realistic fabric behavior, natural folds and believable three-dimensional volume.

COMPOSITION:
Arrange all products together in a visually pleasing and natural way, similar to a professional lifestyle product photograph.

Place the products naturally on the main surface of the provided environment, such as a bed, table, sofa, counter or other surface shown in the reference image.

The products should look physically present in the environment, with realistic contact shadows, natural perspective, correct scale, subtle fabric wrinkles and believable interaction with the surface.

Do NOT simply paste the products onto the background.

The products must be properly integrated into the scene with:
- realistic perspective
- correct lighting direction
- matching exposure
- natural shadows
- realistic ambient light
- accurate depth
- natural reflections when applicable
- physically believable positioning

ENVIRONMENT PRESERVATION:
Keep the original environment, furniture, textures, colors, lighting style and overall visual identity of the scene reference.

Do not unnecessarily modify, replace or redesign the environment.

CAMERA:
Create a realistic smartphone photograph, similar to a high-end iPhone lifestyle product photo.

Natural handheld photography.
Authentic perspective.
Realistic depth of field.
Sharp product details.
Subtle photographic imperfections.
No studio look.
No artificial 3D-rendered appearance.

STYLE:
Ultra-photorealistic.
High-end UGC product photography.
Natural lifestyle aesthetic.
Real fabric texture.
Realistic skin/material texture when hands are present.
Natural shadows.
Professional but authentic.
Photographic realism.
4K/8K quality.

IMPORTANT:
The final image must look like a real person physically placed these exact products in the photographed environment and took a photograph with a smartphone.

Do not generate extra products.
Do not duplicate products.
Do not alter product colors.
Do not change the product design.
Do not add logos, labels or text that are not present in the references.
Do not distort the products.
Do not create AI-looking textures.', NULL),
  ('UGC-002', 'Generate an ultra-realistic vertical UGC product demonstration video (9:16) based on the supplied reference image or first frame.

Preserve the exact same person, product, environment, lighting, clothing, camera perspective, proportions, colors, textures, and overall visual identity throughout the entire video.

ACTION FLOW:

1. OPENING — POINT TO THE SCREEN
The video begins with the person naturally looking toward the camera.
Within the first seconds, the person raises one hand and points with the index finger toward a clearly visible area of the frame, as if directing the viewer''s attention to something on the screen.
The pointing gesture must feel casual, natural, and intentional — like a real TikTok, Reels, or UGC creator.
Keep the hand anatomically correct with five natural fingers, realistic joints, and believable wrist movement.
Do not generate any actual button, interface, text, icon, or graphic where the person points.

2. TRANSITION INTO DEMONSTRATION
Immediately after pointing, the person naturally lowers the hand and begins demonstrating the product.
The transition must happen through continuous human movement, without an artificial cut or sudden pose change.

3. PRODUCT DEMONSTRATION
Show the product being handled and demonstrated in a clear, believable way appropriate to its category.
Use natural grip, realistic pressure, correct scale, and physically believable interaction.
The product must remain 100% visually identical to the reference during the entire video.
Do not redesign, recolor, resize, deform, replace, or invent any part of the product.

PERFORMANCE:
The creator behaves like a real person recording casual social-media content.
Use relaxed body language, subtle facial expressions, natural blinking, small head movements, and realistic eye contact.
Avoid exaggerated influencer gestures or commercial acting.

CAMERA:
Authentic smartphone UGC recording.
Vertical 9:16.
Natural handheld micro-movements.
Subtle push-in or light parallax when useful.
Keep the person and product clearly readable.
No dramatic cinematic camera moves.
No sudden zooms.
No artificial stabilization look.

REALISM:
Natural skin texture and visible pores.
Realistic hands and fingers.
Natural hair movement.
Realistic fabric behavior.
Correct product physics.
Natural indoor or ambient lighting consistent with the reference.
Realistic shadows and reflections.
Maintain visual consistency from first frame to last frame.

AUDIO:
No spoken dialogue by default.
Natural room ambience only.
No music unless specifically requested.

IMPORTANT:
The pointing action is the hook.
The product demonstration is the payoff.
The final result should feel like a genuine creator first catches attention by pointing at the screen and then immediately shows how the product works.

AVOID:
extra fingers, malformed hands, duplicated hands, robotic pointing, floating objects, product morphing, changing product details, flickering, sudden scene changes, fake interface elements, generated text, buttons, logos, watermarks, exaggerated acting, CGI look, 3D-rendered appearance.', NULL),
  ('UGC-003', 'Generate an ultra-realistic vertical UGC fashion video (9:16) based on the supplied reference image or first frame.

Preserve the exact same woman, face, body, hairstyle, clothing product, colors, fabric, print, stitching, proportions, environment, lighting, camera perspective, and overall visual identity throughout the entire video.

MAIN ACTION — SHOW THE BLOUSE:

The woman naturally presents the blouse she is wearing to the camera, like a real social-media creator showing the fit and details of a clothing item.

Begin with the woman facing the camera in a relaxed position.

She gently brings both hands toward the blouse and naturally holds the fabric near the lower sides or hem.

She lightly pulls the blouse outward just enough to show its width, cut, drape, and fabric movement without stretching or deforming the garment.

Then she releases one side naturally and softly runs one hand over a small section of the blouse, highlighting the material, texture, print, stitching, or fit.

She makes a subtle torso movement or small body turn to help show how the blouse fits from another angle.

End with the blouse clearly visible and the woman in a natural relaxed pose.

PRODUCT PRESERVATION:

The blouse must remain 100% identical to the reference image during the entire video.

Preserve exactly:
• original color
• print or pattern
• neckline
• sleeves
• seams
• stitching
• fabric texture
• length
• cut
• proportions
• logos or details that genuinely exist in the reference

Do not redesign the blouse.
Do not change its color.
Do not change the neckline or sleeves.
Do not add or remove prints.
Do not make the blouse tighter, looser, shorter, or longer.
Do not transform it into another garment.

FABRIC PHYSICS:

Fabric movement must be subtle and physically realistic.
When the hands touch or lightly pull the blouse, create believable tension, folds, wrinkles, weight, and recovery.
The fabric must never move by itself or behave like rubber.

HAND AND BODY REALISM:

Maintain anatomically correct hands and fingers.
Natural grip and realistic finger pressure on the fabric.
No extra fingers.
No duplicated hands.
No distorted wrists.
No unnatural body deformation.

The woman''s movements should feel casual, confident, and effortless, like an authentic TikTok, Reels, or UGC fashion demonstration.

CAMERA:

Vertical 9:16 smartphone recording.
Natural handheld micro-movements.
Mostly stable framing centered on the woman and blouse.
Subtle push-in or slight reframing is allowed to emphasize clothing details.
No dramatic zooms.
No cinematic orbit.
No sudden camera movement.

REALISTIC APPEARANCE:

Natural skin texture and pores.
Realistic hair movement.
Natural fabric texture.
Soft realistic shadows.
Balanced exposure.
True-to-life colors.
Authentic smartphone depth of field.
No excessive beauty filter.
No artificial cinematic grading.

AUDIO:

No spoken dialogue by default.
Natural room ambience only.
No music unless specifically requested.

FINAL RESULT:

A believable UGC fashion video where the woman naturally uses her hands and subtle body movement to show the exact blouse, its fit, fabric, and details, as if she were casually presenting the clothing item to her followers.

AVOID:

product morphing, changing clothing design, changing colors, changing print, distorted fabric, rubber-like fabric, extra fingers, malformed hands, duplicated hands, body deformation, exaggerated posing, robotic movement, flickering, sudden scene changes, generated text, fake interface elements, watermarks, CGI look, 3D-rendered appearance.', NULL),
  ('LANCAMENTO-001', 'Create a 15-second ultra-realistic vertical 9:16 viral social-media video using TWO reference images.

REFERENCE IMAGE 1 — CHARACTER:
Use Image 1 as the absolute identity reference for the character. Preserve the exact face, facial proportions, skin tone, hairstyle, hairline, body proportions, age appearance, tattoos, accessories and other visible identity details. The same person must remain recognizable and consistent from the first frame to the last frame.

REFERENCE IMAGE 2 — LOCATION:
Use Image 2 as the absolute environment reference. Reconstruct the same real location with the same architecture, ground, objects, vehicles or furniture when present, horizon, lighting direction, colors, spatial layout and overall atmosphere. Do not replace the location with a generic environment.

CORE CONCEPT:
Create a fast, energetic viral character-showcase video inspired by authentic smartphone / 360 action-camera social content. The character performs casual rhythmic movements and confident gestures while the edit rapidly alternates between full-body views, extreme close-ups, low-angle selfie-style shots and wider environmental reveals.

The result must feel like one real person recorded multiple short moments in the same location and they were edited together to the beat.

SHOT SEQUENCE:

SHOT 1 — 0.0–3.5s — FULL-BODY PERFORMANCE
Show the character standing several feet from the camera in the referenced location.
Use an ultra-wide smartphone/action-camera perspective with slight natural fisheye distortion.
The character performs energetic but believable rhythmic movement: light footwork, small side-to-side steps, shoulder movement and expressive arm gestures.
Keep the body anatomically correct and the motion physically realistic.
The camera remains close enough to feel immersive and moves subtly with handheld energy.

SHOT 2 — 3.5–5.5s — LOW-ANGLE SELFIE HIT
Use a fast match cut or whip-style transition into a dramatic close selfie-style angle.
The character is much closer to the lens, slightly low-angle, with one arm naturally extended toward the camera as if holding an invisible action camera or selfie stick outside the visible frame.
The character smiles or makes a confident playful hand gesture.
Use strong ultra-wide perspective, but never distort the face beyond natural action-camera optics.

SHOT 3 — 5.5–8.0s — EXTREME FACE CLOSE-UP
Cut to a close facial shot.
The character makes subtle natural expressions: blinking, a small smile, slight head movement and relaxed eye contact.
Preserve real skin texture, pores and facial identity.
Allow warm environmental light to interact naturally with the face.

SHOT 4 — 8.0–9.5s — ENVIRONMENT REVEAL
Quickly pull or cut back to a wide view of the referenced location.
Show the environment clearly for a brief moment.
The character may appear farther away, entering the frame, standing beside a real object from the scene, or naturally repositioning within the environment.
Preserve exact scene geometry and object permanence.

SHOT 5 — 9.5–12.0s — MEDIUM / FULL-BODY HERO MOMENT
Show the same character again in a medium or full-body shot.
The character faces the camera with relaxed confidence, subtle rhythmic body movement and a natural social-media presence.
Use realistic posture, weight shifts and hand movement.

SHOT 6 — 12.0–14.0s — ENERGETIC CALLBACK
Use another fast match cut back to an energetic ultra-wide full-body angle.
The character performs one final expressive arm gesture or short dance movement that visually echoes the opening.
Camera motion can include a subtle orbit, tilt or handheld swing synchronized with the movement.

SHOT 7 — 14.0–15.0s — FINAL CLOSE-UP
Finish with a quick close-up or medium close-up of the same character smiling or looking directly into the camera.
End on a clean, confident frame suitable for a seamless social-media edit.

EDITING LANGUAGE:
Use approximately 6–7 short shots across 15 seconds.
Cuts should feel synchronized to the rhythm of the soundtrack.
Use hard cuts, match cuts and very short whip transitions.
Alternate visual scale aggressively: full body → close-up → wide environment → close-up → full body.
Keep the pacing fast but readable.
Do not use artificial morph transitions.
Do not transform the character during cuts.

CAMERA:
Vertical 9:16.
Authentic high-end smartphone / 360 action-camera aesthetic.
Ultra-wide 14–18mm equivalent look for the dynamic full-body shots.
24mm smartphone look for closer portrait moments.
Natural fisheye perspective only when appropriate.
Handheld micro-movement.
Fast physical reframing.
Occasional slight camera tilt.
Realistic motion blur during fast transitions.
No floating camera.
No impossible drone movement.

CHARACTER CONSISTENCY:
The character from Image 1 must remain the SAME person in every shot.
Do not change face, hairstyle, body, skin tone or age.
Do not generate multiple different people.
Do not clone the character inside the same frame.
Clothing should remain consistent unless additional clothing references are explicitly supplied.

LOCATION CONSISTENCY:
All shots happen inside the environment from Image 2.
Different camera positions are allowed, but they must remain spatially believable parts of the same location.
Preserve important background landmarks and objects so viewers recognize the environment across cuts.

PHYSICAL REALISM:
Natural human balance.
Correct hands and fingers.
Realistic feet and ground contact.
Realistic fabric movement.
Realistic hair movement.
Natural shadows.
Consistent sunset/daylight/indoor lighting based entirely on Image 2.
Correct reflections and perspective.
No body warping.

AUDIO:
Use an energetic rhythmic social-media soundtrack if the generation system supports audio.
Edit visual cuts and body movement to the beat.
No spoken dialogue by default.
No lip-sync unless dialogue is specifically added by the user.

FINAL STYLE:
Viral TikTok/Reels energy.
Authentic spontaneous performance.
Dynamic 360/action-camera feeling.
Real person, real location, real smartphone capture.
Fast, fun, immersive and highly shareable.
Not a polished commercial.

AVOID:
identity drift, face changing between shots, different people, duplicate character, extra limbs, extra fingers, malformed hands, body morphing, floating feet, location changing, invented buildings or vehicles, background morphing, teleportation inside a continuous shot, rubber-like motion, robotic dance, excessive fisheye distortion, artificial CGI, 3D-rendered appearance, beauty-filter skin, generated text, subtitles, logos, watermarks.', NULL),
  ('LANCAMENTO-004', 'Create an ultra-realistic vertical 9:16 viral social-media sequence using THREE character reference images and ONE location reference image.

REFERENCE IMAGE 1 — CHARACTER A:
Use as the absolute identity reference for the first character.

REFERENCE IMAGE 2 — CHARACTER B:
Use as the absolute identity reference for the second character.

REFERENCE IMAGE 3 — CHARACTER C:
Use as the absolute identity reference for the third character.

REFERENCE IMAGE 4 — LOCATION:
Use as the absolute reference for the outdoor environment, architecture, pavement, parked vehicles, sky, sunset lighting, horizon, colors and spatial layout.

CORE CONCEPT:
Create a fast viral encounter montage in one continuous location. Character A begins with an energetic full-body performance. A fast camera handoff reveals Character B in a close ultra-wide selfie-style shot. The camera then leaves Character B and reveals the environment and parked vehicles. Character C emerges naturally near a vehicle, is shown first in a close portrait and then in a full-body view. A final whip-pan returns to Character B for the ending.

The complete reference sequence is approximately 18 seconds and should be generated as THREE connected takes of approximately 6 seconds each.

CONTINUITY METHOD:
Generate Take 1 first.
Export the exact final frame from Take 1 and use it as the starting-frame reference for Take 2.
Export the exact final frame from Take 2 and use it as the starting-frame reference for Take 3.
If only one section is desired, generate only that take with the required references.

IDENTITY LOCK:
Character A, Character B and Character C must remain three clearly different people.
Never blend their faces, hairstyles, bodies, clothing or accessories.
Never morph one person into another.
Character changes happen only through real camera movement, cuts, whip pans or environmental occlusion.

LOCATION LOCK:
Every shot must remain in the same referenced location.
Preserve important vehicles, buildings, pavement, horizon, sunset direction and scene geometry.
Different camera positions are allowed only when they remain spatially believable.

CAMERA STYLE:
Authentic smartphone / action-camera social video.
Vertical 9:16.
Ultra-wide perspective for energetic full-body and selfie shots.
24mm-style perspective for close portraits.
Natural handheld movement.
Whip pans with real directional motion blur.
Fast but believable reframing.
No floating camera.
No impossible orbit.

REALISM:
Natural skin texture.
Correct hands and fingers.
Realistic feet-to-ground contact.
Natural clothing and hair physics.
Consistent sunset lighting.
Realistic reflections on vehicles.
True-to-life smartphone exposure.
No CGI or video-game appearance.

AUDIO:
No spoken dialogue by default.
Natural ambience or rhythmic social-media soundtrack if supported.
No generated subtitles.

AVOID:
identity drift, face blending, face morphing, duplicated characters, extra limbs, malformed hands, background replacement, changing vehicles, teleportation without camera transition, artificial morph transitions, excessive fisheye distortion, CGI, 3D render, generated text, subtitles, logos, watermarks.', '[{"take":1,"duration":"~6s","title":"Personagem A → Selfie do Personagem B","prompt":"TAKE 1 — ~6 SECONDS — CHARACTER A PERFORMANCE → CHARACTER B SELFIE\n\nUse Character A, Character B and Location references.\n\n0.0–3.8s:\nShow Character A several feet from the camera in a full-body ultra-wide view in the referenced outdoor location.\nCharacter A performs relaxed rhythmic movement: small steps, shoulder movement and expressive arm gestures.\nThe camera gradually moves closer with authentic handheld action-camera energy.\n\n3.8–4.5s:\nCharacter A raises the arms toward the upper frame, creating a natural transition cue.\nUse fast physical camera movement and realistic motion blur.\nDo NOT morph Character A into Character B.\n\n4.5–6.0s:\nReveal Character B through a hard/match cut or whip transition.\nCharacter B is now close to the lens in a low-angle ultra-wide selfie-style composition, smiling and making a playful hand gesture.\nKeep Character B exactly consistent with its reference.\n\nEND:\nFinish with Character B close to the lens in a stable recognizable pose.\nExport this exact final frame for Take 2.\n\nNo dialogue.\nNo text.\nNo face morphing."},{"take":2,"duration":"~6s","title":"Personagem B → Cenário → Close do Personagem C","prompt":"TAKE 2 — ~6 SECONDS — CHARACTER B → ENVIRONMENT → CHARACTER C CLOSE-UP\n\nSTARTING FRAME:\nUse the exact final frame exported from Take 1.\n\n0.0–1.0s:\nContinue Character B''s exact pose and camera position naturally for a brief moment.\n\n1.0–2.0s:\nThe camera performs a fast physical whip-pan away from Character B.\nUse authentic directional motion blur.\n\n2.0–3.0s:\nReveal the referenced environment and parked vehicles.\nThe frame briefly focuses on the real location rather than a person.\nPreserve vehicle positions and scene geometry.\n\n3.0–4.0s:\nCharacter C naturally appears from beside or inside one of the vehicles.\nThe entrance must be physically believable.\n\n4.0–6.0s:\nMove into a close portrait of Character C.\nCharacter C looks toward the camera with subtle natural expressions, blinking and a relaxed smile.\nPreserve exact face, hair, clothing and accessories from Character C''s reference.\n\nEND:\nFinish on a stable close-up of Character C.\nExport this exact final frame for Take 3.\n\nNo dialogue.\nNo generated text.\nNo identity blending."},{"take":3,"duration":"~6s","title":"Personagem C → Whip Pan → Personagem B","prompt":"TAKE 3 — ~6 SECONDS — CHARACTER C FULL BODY → WHIP PAN → CHARACTER B ENDING\n\nSTARTING FRAME:\nUse the exact final frame exported from Take 2.\n\n0.0–3.0s:\nCamera naturally pulls back from Character C''s close-up into a medium and then full-body view beside the same parked vehicles.\nCharacter C smiles and performs relaxed natural body language.\nMaintain exact clothing, identity, lighting and location.\n\n3.0–4.0s:\nPerform a fast whip-pan away from Character C.\nUse real directional camera movement and natural motion blur.\nDo not morph Character C into another person.\n\n4.0–6.0s:\nReveal Character B again in a close low-angle smartphone/selfie view in the same location.\nCharacter B looks upward or toward the camera with a relaxed confident expression.\nCharacter A may remain naturally visible far in the background only if spatially appropriate, but must not be duplicated or become the focal subject.\n\nEND:\nFinish cleanly on Character B with the sunset/location still recognizable.\n\nNo dialogue.\nNo generated subtitles or text.\nNo morph transition.\nNo CGI look."}]'::jsonb),
  ('LANCAMENTO-005', 'Create an ultra-realistic vertical 9:16 cinematic chase sequence using the supplied character and location references.

REFERENCE IMAGE 1 — MAIN CHARACTER:
Use Image 1 as the absolute identity reference for the person being chased. Preserve the exact face, age appearance, skin tone, hairstyle, body proportions, clothing and accessories throughout the sequence.

REFERENCE IMAGE 2 — LOCATION:
Use Image 2 as the absolute environment reference. Preserve the same roads, ramps, barriers, buildings, lighting direction, sky, colors and spatial identity while adapting the route naturally for the chase.

OPTIONAL IMAGE 3 — PURSUER / RIDER:
If supplied, use it as the identity reference for the rider. If it is not supplied, create a visually consistent secondary rider without replacing or blending with the main character.

OPTIONAL IMAGE 4 — MOTORCYCLE / VEHICLE:
If supplied, preserve the exact vehicle design. Otherwise create one consistent futuristic motorcycle and keep that same vehicle throughout.

CORE CONCEPT:
A fast cinematic pursuit. The main character moves urgently through an elevated urban roadway while a rider on a futuristic motorcycle approaches from behind. Alternate between close tracking shots of the main character, a dramatic frontal rider shot, a near-collision/evasive action beat and a fast motorcycle chase POV. End by returning to a close tracking view of the main character.

The reference sequence is approximately 17 seconds. Generate it as TWO connected takes:
Take 1 = approximately 10 seconds.
Take 2 = approximately 7 seconds.

CONTINUITY:
Generate Take 1 first.
Export the exact final frame of Take 1.
Use that exact frame as the starting-frame reference for Take 2.
Do not recreate the transition frame from memory.

If the user wants only one part, generate only the desired take.

REALISM:
Ultra-photorealistic live-action look.
Real human anatomy.
Correct hands and fingers.
Natural clothing movement.
Believable balance and ground contact.
Consistent character identity.
Consistent motorcycle design.
Realistic road perspective.
Natural sunset/city lighting.
Realistic vehicle speed, suspension and motion blur.

CAMERA:
Vertical 9:16.
Low-angle action-camera and smartphone-style tracking.
Fast forward motion.
Occasional frontal tracking.
Motorcycle chase POV.
Natural camera shake caused by speed.
Strong but physically believable motion blur.
No impossible floating camera.

AVOID:
identity drift, face morphing, duplicate main character, character blending, changing clothing, changing motorcycle design, duplicated motorcycle, extra limbs, malformed hands, broken body anatomy, body passing through vehicle, impossible vehicle physics, background morphing, teleportation without a cut, fake CGI, video-game look, generated text, subtitles, logos and watermarks.', '[{"take":1,"duration":"10s","title":"Fuga → Moto → Desvio","prompt":"TAKE 1 — 10 SECONDS — MAIN CHARACTER + RIDER REVEAL + EVASIVE ACTION\n\nUse:\nImage 1 = main character.\nImage 2 = location.\nOptional Image 3 = rider.\nOptional Image 4 = motorcycle.\n\n0.0–4.2s:\nBegin with the main character moving quickly toward/along the camera on the referenced elevated urban roadway.\nUse a low, slightly wide frontal tracking angle.\nThe character leans forward with urgent, energetic body language while the camera travels backward at matching speed.\nKeep the face clearly recognizable and stable.\nA motorcycle may become visible in the distance behind the character.\n\n4.2–5.8s:\nCut to a dramatic frontal close shot of the pursuing rider on the motorcycle.\nThe rider grips the handlebars naturally.\nThe motorcycle advances rapidly through the same environment.\nUse realistic wind, clothing movement, suspension and road motion.\n\n5.8–7.7s:\nReturn to the main character with the motorcycle now visibly closing the distance from behind.\nThe character notices the threat and changes body position naturally while continuing forward.\n\n7.7–9.2s:\nCreate a dramatic evasive action beat as the motorcycle approaches.\nThe main character ducks, rolls, jumps aside or performs a believable emergency dodge without being struck.\nKeep anatomy physically coherent.\nThe motorcycle passes through the scene without intersecting the body.\n\n9.2–10.0s:\nTransition into a wider chase view down the roadway, establishing the motorcycle continuing after the main character.\n\nEND FRAME:\nFinish with the road direction, main character and/or pursuing motorcycle clearly oriented for forward continuation.\nThe final frame must be stable enough to become the exact starting image for Take 2.\n\nNo dialogue.\nNo generated text.\nNo face morphing.\nNo collision gore or injury."},{"take":2,"duration":"7s","title":"Perseguição POV → Close final","prompt":"TAKE 2 — 7 SECONDS — MOTORCYCLE CHASE POV + FINAL CHARACTER CLOSE-UP\n\nSTARTING FRAME:\nUse the exact final frame exported from Take 1.\nStart with zero visual jump.\nPreserve the same roadway, direction of travel, lighting, character identity, motorcycle design and chase momentum.\n\n0.0–3.8s:\nShift naturally into a fast chase perspective following the motorcycle and the main character through the curved elevated roadway.\nUse a low motorcycle/action-camera feeling with strong forward movement.\nRoad barriers, buildings and environmental landmarks move past with realistic parallax.\nThe motorcycle follows the curve naturally and maintains believable tire contact and lean.\n\n3.8–5.2s:\nThe camera closes the distance again.\nReveal the main character ahead while the motorcycle remains part of the pursuit.\nUse realistic speed and directional motion blur.\n\n5.2–7.0s:\nTransition back to a close frontal/three-quarter tracking view of the main character moving quickly.\nThe character looks energized and reactive while maintaining the exact same identity, clothing and appearance.\nKeep the urban chase environment recognizable behind the character.\n\nEND:\nFinish on a strong close tracking hero frame of the main character, with the pursuit context still readable in the background.\n\nNo dialogue.\nNo generated text.\nNo body deformation.\nNo motorcycle duplication.\nNo CGI or video-game appearance."}]'::jsonb),
  ('LANCAMENTO-006', 'Create an ultra-realistic vertical 9:16 viral music-performance video using TWO character reference images and ONE optional environment reference.

REFERENCE IMAGE 1 — CHARACTER A:
Use as the absolute identity reference for the performer on the left. Preserve the exact face, age appearance, skin tone, hairstyle, body proportions, clothing/accessories and all recognizable identity details.

REFERENCE IMAGE 2 — CHARACTER B:
Use as the absolute identity reference for the performer on the right. Preserve the exact face, age appearance, skin tone, hairstyle, body proportions, clothing/accessories and all recognizable identity details.

OPTIONAL IMAGE 3 — STUDIO / BACKGROUND:
If supplied, preserve that environment. If not supplied, use a simple clean warm-color studio backdrop with a single hanging condenser microphone centered above/between the two performers.

CORE CONCEPT:
Two distinct characters perform together under one suspended studio microphone in a playful viral music-session style. Both remain visible for almost the entire sequence. They alternate attention toward the microphone and each other, gesture rhythmically with their hands, react to each other''s performance and move naturally to the beat. Character B becomes more animated in the second half with a short dance/body-turn moment before both performers reconnect near the microphone for the ending.

The complete reference sequence is approximately 15 seconds.
Generate as TWO connected takes:
Take 1 = 10 seconds.
Take 2 = 5 seconds.

CONTINUITY:
Generate Take 1 first.
Export the exact final frame from Take 1.
Use that exact final frame as the starting-frame reference for Take 2.
Start Take 2 with zero visual jump.

If the user wants only one section, generate only the desired take.

PERFORMANCE:
Natural rhythmic body movement.
Believable mouth movement as if singing/rapping along to a track.
Expressive hand gestures.
Natural eye contact between performers.
Occasional attention toward the hanging microphone.
Relaxed playful chemistry.
No fighting or aggressive physical contact.

AUDIO:
Do not invent intelligible lyrics or dialogue by default.
The visual performance should be suitable for adding the user''s music/audio afterward.
If the generation tool supports a supplied audio track, synchronize mouth and body rhythm to that supplied track without changing character identity.

CAMERA:
Mostly fixed medium-wide studio framing.
Both performers remain readable.
Very subtle handheld or stabilized micro-movement only.
No unnecessary cuts.
No orbiting camera.
No dramatic zooms.
Preserve the hanging microphone position.

REALISM:
Ultra-photorealistic live-action.
Natural skin texture.
Correct hands and fingers.
Realistic muscles/body anatomy.
Natural breathing.
Believable shoulder, elbow and wrist motion.
Realistic clothing/accessory physics.
Consistent lighting and shadows.

AVOID:
identity drift, face blending, one character transforming into the other, duplicate performers, extra limbs, malformed fingers, broken anatomy, bodies intersecting, disappearing microphone, moving microphone without physical reason, random wardrobe changes, background morphing, artificial face morphs, CGI, cartoon look, generated text, subtitles, logos and watermarks.', '[{"take":1,"duration":"10s","title":"Dueto → Reações → Dança","prompt":"TAKE 1 — 10 SECONDS — DUET PERFORMANCE + RHYTHMIC REACTIONS\n\nUse:\nImage 1 = Character A.\nImage 2 = Character B.\nOptional Image 3 = studio/background.\n\nPlace both performers side by side beneath one suspended condenser microphone.\n\n0.0–2.5s:\nCharacter A leans slightly toward the hanging microphone and performs as if singing/rapping.\nCharacter B watches, reacts naturally and points/gestures rhythmically toward Character A or the beat.\nBoth maintain subtle body bounce.\n\n2.5–5.0s:\nBoth performers remain in frame.\nCharacter A continues expressive mouth movement and small hand gestures.\nCharacter B opens the hands and gestures conversationally, as if responding to the performance.\nThey briefly look toward each other, creating believable chemistry.\n\n5.0–7.5s:\nCharacter B becomes more active, gesturing with one hand near the chest and the other outward.\nCharacter A remains engaged near the microphone, nodding and moving to the rhythm.\n\n7.5–10.0s:\nCharacter B transitions into a more energetic dance move, rotating the torso partly away from camera while moving the arms rhythmically.\nCharacter A stays near the microphone, clapping lightly or marking the rhythm with the hands.\n\nEND FRAME:\nFinish with Character B partly turned and mid-dance while Character A remains clearly visible near the microphone.\nHold the final fraction of a second stable enough to export as the exact starting frame for Take 2.\n\nNo invented intelligible lyrics.\nNo subtitles.\nNo character morphing."},{"take":2,"duration":"5s","title":"Continuação da dança → Final no microfone","prompt":"TAKE 2 — 5 SECONDS — CONTINUE DANCE → BOTH RECONNECT AT MICROPHONE\n\nSTARTING FRAME:\nUse the exact final frame exported from Take 1.\nBegin with zero visual jump.\nPreserve both characters'' exact poses, identities, clothing, microphone position, background, lighting and camera framing.\n\n0.0–1.5s:\nCharacter B naturally completes the dance/body-turn movement and rotates back toward Character A and the microphone.\nCharacter A continues marking the rhythm with small hand gestures.\n\n1.5–3.5s:\nThe two performers move slightly closer toward the central microphone and interact playfully.\nThey gesture toward each other as if trading lines in a duet.\nUse believable eye contact, facial expressions and rhythmic mouth movement.\n\n3.5–5.0s:\nBoth face more toward the microphone/camera.\nCharacter A makes a final expressive hand gesture while Character B responds with a playful gesture near the head/face.\nEnd with both characters clearly visible together under the microphone.\n\nNo invented intelligible lyrics.\nNo generated text.\nNo identity blending.\nNo camera teleport.\nNo CGI look."}]'::jsonb)
